1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:01,196
A few months ago,

2
00:00:01,196 --> 00:00:06,876
180 of us came together to build something in Singapore:

3
00:00:06,876 --> 00:00:08,436
a library.

4
00:00:08,436 --> 00:00:11,876
In a city where we're lucky enough to already have a world-class,

5
00:00:11,876 --> 00:00:16,356
highly innovative, much beloved public library system,

6
00:00:16,356 --> 00:00:19,436
of which I am a happy beneficiary,

7
00:00:19,436 --> 00:00:22,636
how is our library different?

8
00:00:22,636 --> 00:00:24,756
So this is a picture of the library we built.

9
00:00:24,756 --> 00:00:27,196
We are nestled in the heartlands of Singapore,

10
00:00:27,196 --> 00:00:30,116
where we sit between a 50-year-old furniture store

11
00:00:30,116 --> 00:00:31,516
and an antique shop,

12
00:00:31,516 --> 00:00:36,716
surrounded by birds and trees and ...

13
00:00:36,716 --> 00:00:38,316
Quite a few chickens.

14
00:00:38,316 --> 00:00:40,756
(Laughter)

15
00:00:40,756 --> 00:00:43,556
So our library is what we call a shared library.

16
00:00:43,556 --> 00:00:46,316
There are 180 shelves in our library,

17
00:00:46,316 --> 00:00:50,796
each of them rented and maintained by a different person.

18
00:00:50,796 --> 00:00:52,996
For 45 Singapore dollars a month

19
00:00:52,996 --> 00:00:56,516
you get to become a bookshelf owner.

20
00:00:56,516 --> 00:00:58,756
Being a bookshelf owner is actually really fun,

21
00:00:58,756 --> 00:01:01,716
because you get to share your favorite books on your own shelf,

22
00:01:01,716 --> 00:01:04,116
you get to decorate your own space

23
00:01:04,116 --> 00:01:08,356
and change up your books every now and then.

24
00:01:08,356 --> 00:01:12,636
Every shelf in our library is like a little pocket universe,

25
00:01:12,636 --> 00:01:19,036
an interesting, almost voyeuristic glimpse into someone's heart and mind.

26
00:01:19,036 --> 00:01:20,716
And if you walk around our library,

27
00:01:20,716 --> 00:01:23,236
quite often you end up standing in front of a shelf

28
00:01:23,236 --> 00:01:26,636
thinking to yourself, "I think I know this person,"

29
00:01:26,636 --> 00:01:32,356
even though, strangely, you've never met this person in your entire life.

30
00:01:32,356 --> 00:01:34,516
The other interesting thing about our library

31
00:01:34,516 --> 00:01:36,756
is that we are entirely volunteer-run.

32
00:01:36,756 --> 00:01:41,316
So a roster of about 40 very kind volunteer librarians

33
00:01:41,316 --> 00:01:47,556
take turns to keep our library open six days a week, 12 hours a day.

34
00:01:47,556 --> 00:01:50,156
Some of them are bookshop owners,

35
00:01:50,156 --> 00:01:51,796
they have a shelf in our library.

36
00:01:51,796 --> 00:01:53,836
Some of them are people from the community

37
00:01:53,836 --> 00:01:57,076
who really want to be part of the project.

38
00:01:57,076 --> 00:02:02,796
They are doctors, ex-lawyers, students, mothers, freelancers, retirees.

39
00:02:02,796 --> 00:02:06,596
We even have a five-year-old librarian among us.

40
00:02:06,596 --> 00:02:08,356
But we all have something in common.

41
00:02:08,356 --> 00:02:10,996
We love books, we believe in the power of books,

42
00:02:10,996 --> 00:02:14,996
and we enjoy being surrounded by books.

43
00:02:14,996 --> 00:02:18,476
So any time we’re open, anyone is welcome to come in,

44
00:02:18,476 --> 00:02:21,116
to sit down, to read a book for free,

45
00:02:21,116 --> 00:02:23,756
to make a friend or connection,

46
00:02:23,756 --> 00:02:28,796
to drink a cup of coffee, randomly brewed by someone in the library,

47
00:02:28,796 --> 00:02:33,036
and to just let the stress of life melt off their shoulders.

48
00:02:33,036 --> 00:02:38,276
So how did we come to build a library like this?

49
00:02:38,276 --> 00:02:39,556
So in April this year,

50
00:02:39,556 --> 00:02:41,716
I took a break from my work as a photographer,

51
00:02:41,716 --> 00:02:44,276
and I went to Japan for a sabbatical,

52
00:02:44,276 --> 00:02:50,636
where I stumbled upon a small, shared community library with a similar concept.

53
00:02:50,636 --> 00:02:55,276
I loved it. I loved it.

54
00:02:55,276 --> 00:03:00,756
There was just something about the space that really enticed me.

55
00:03:00,756 --> 00:03:02,876
Standing in that little library in Japan,

56
00:03:02,876 --> 00:03:06,316
even though I couldn't read any of the Japanese books,

57
00:03:06,316 --> 00:03:11,076
I could sense a very powerful energy of love and care.

58
00:03:11,076 --> 00:03:14,476
And even though I couldn't read any of the Japanese books there,

59
00:03:14,476 --> 00:03:16,756
I thought it would be wonderful if we could have

60
00:03:16,756 --> 00:03:18,396
something like this in Singapore.

61
00:03:18,396 --> 00:03:21,236
So I decided to turn to social media.

62
00:03:21,236 --> 00:03:24,036
So I came back to Singapore,

63
00:03:24,036 --> 00:03:27,316
decided to write a simple Instagram post,

64
00:03:27,316 --> 00:03:29,836
just outlining my vision of what I thought

65
00:03:29,836 --> 00:03:34,116
a small, shared library in Singapore could look like.

66
00:03:34,116 --> 00:03:35,636
I thought if no one responded,

67
00:03:35,636 --> 00:03:37,876
then I would just forget about the whole thing.

68
00:03:37,876 --> 00:03:40,596
But if I could somehow get 50 people on board,

69
00:03:40,596 --> 00:03:43,156
I would be happy to give this very impractical,

70
00:03:43,156 --> 00:03:47,316
dreamy and idealistic idea a try.

71
00:03:47,316 --> 00:03:49,556
So I quickly finished writing, pressed post,

72
00:03:49,556 --> 00:03:52,996
and then I went back to work.

73
00:03:52,996 --> 00:03:54,636
By the end of the first day,

74
00:03:54,636 --> 00:03:57,396
I had received about 300 direct messages

75
00:03:57,396 --> 00:03:59,876
from people who said they were seriously interested

76
00:03:59,876 --> 00:04:02,156
in putting money down to rent a shelf

77
00:04:02,156 --> 00:04:05,516
and to build a library together.

78
00:04:05,516 --> 00:04:07,116
I was quite emotional that night

79
00:04:07,116 --> 00:04:09,836
because I could really sense people's passion

80
00:04:09,836 --> 00:04:12,316
and fire in their messages.

81
00:04:12,316 --> 00:04:16,916
Living in Singapore, where, you know, life is practical and pragmatic,

82
00:04:16,916 --> 00:04:19,756
this felt almost like a jailbreak kind of moment,

83
00:04:19,756 --> 00:04:22,916
and I think a lot of people were just waiting for the right time

84
00:04:22,916 --> 00:04:26,916
and the right chance to do something like this together.

85
00:04:26,916 --> 00:04:29,476
I guess I was also really moved by the realization

86
00:04:29,476 --> 00:04:32,436
that so many people cared about books.

87
00:04:32,436 --> 00:04:35,396
So I took a leap of faith.

88
00:04:35,396 --> 00:04:38,516
I found a space to rent on the third day of the Instagram post.

89
00:04:38,516 --> 00:04:42,596
I signed the lease on the fourth day.

90
00:04:42,596 --> 00:04:44,116
I'm an Aquarius, so.

91
00:04:44,116 --> 00:04:45,276
(Laughter)

92
00:04:45,276 --> 00:04:48,436
With the help of many people, most of them strangers,

93
00:04:48,436 --> 00:04:51,796
we built the library in three months.

94
00:04:51,796 --> 00:04:54,756
This is a picture of us literally building the library.

95
00:04:54,756 --> 00:04:58,436
Here, we were installing the flooring together.

96
00:04:58,436 --> 00:05:04,316
So who are some of these 180 crazy people who decided to do this with me?

97
00:05:04,316 --> 00:05:08,356
So this is Raymond's shelf.

98
00:05:08,356 --> 00:05:11,516
Raymond loves collecting CDs and books about Japan --

99
00:05:11,516 --> 00:05:14,756
so these are the two things that you can find on his shelf.

100
00:05:14,756 --> 00:05:18,876
Every Tuesday, Raymond sets up a little stall outside our library

101
00:05:18,876 --> 00:05:21,356
where he sells his secondhand CDs and books.

102
00:05:21,356 --> 00:05:23,996
He also brews coffee -- pay as you wish --

103
00:05:23,996 --> 00:05:28,116
for anyone who happens to drop by.

104
00:05:28,116 --> 00:05:30,636
This is Wanqi and Liam, the mother and son.

105
00:05:30,636 --> 00:05:35,036
Liam is also the five-year-old librarian I was talking about earlier.

106
00:05:35,036 --> 00:05:38,156
So far, we've been quite impressed by his work ethic.

107
00:05:38,156 --> 00:05:39,636
(Laughter)

108
00:05:39,636 --> 00:05:41,796
And their shelf is split half-half,

109
00:05:41,796 --> 00:05:44,796
half between Liam's favorite children's books,

110
00:05:44,796 --> 00:05:46,996
and half between Wanqi's books.

111
00:05:46,996 --> 00:05:49,836
Liam was also responsible for decorating their shelf.

112
00:05:49,836 --> 00:05:51,796
It looks great.

113
00:05:51,796 --> 00:05:53,476
So this particular bookshelf owner,

114
00:05:53,476 --> 00:05:55,516
she has decided to share some of the books

115
00:05:55,516 --> 00:05:58,356
that used to be on the shelves of Kin San,

116
00:05:58,356 --> 00:06:02,276
which is a Hong Kong bookshop that has unfortunately closed.

117
00:06:02,276 --> 00:06:05,356
She has also replicated a miniature version of the banner

118
00:06:05,356 --> 00:06:11,116
that used to hang in front of Kin San that says "ideas are bulletproof."

119
00:06:11,116 --> 00:06:16,196
In a way, Kin San lives on right here inside our library.

120
00:06:16,196 --> 00:06:18,236
This particular bookshop owner,

121
00:06:18,236 --> 00:06:20,996
her name is Mira, is a preschool teacher.

122
00:06:20,996 --> 00:06:24,676
She believes in the power of letter writing,

123
00:06:24,676 --> 00:06:27,116
especially with children,

124
00:06:27,116 --> 00:06:30,956
which is why you can find a mailbox on her shelf.

125
00:06:30,956 --> 00:06:34,236
So apart from reading the amazing selection of books on her shelf,

126
00:06:34,236 --> 00:06:36,436
you can also write her a letter.

127
00:06:36,436 --> 00:06:40,916
She has promised that she'll definitely write you back.

128
00:06:40,916 --> 00:06:46,036
So one of the most frequent questions we get asked is

129
00:06:46,036 --> 00:06:47,796
how does this library stay open?

130
00:06:47,796 --> 00:06:51,756
It doesn't really make economic sense, right?

131
00:06:51,756 --> 00:06:53,436
I think the simple answer is

132
00:06:53,436 --> 00:06:58,876
because 180 people have decided that it is possible to come together

133
00:06:58,876 --> 00:07:02,756
to build a ground-up book space for the local community.

134
00:07:02,756 --> 00:07:07,076
And as it turns out, this is actually quite a practical solution

135
00:07:07,076 --> 00:07:11,716
because this means that we're able to share the rent among 180 people,

136
00:07:11,716 --> 00:07:15,396
and no one person has to bear the financial burden

137
00:07:15,396 --> 00:07:18,796
of keeping this space open.

138
00:07:18,796 --> 00:07:21,156
The other question we get asked a lot is,

139
00:07:21,156 --> 00:07:24,156
what do you get out of doing this?

140
00:07:24,156 --> 00:07:27,196
Well, we definitely don't get paid in the traditional sense,

141
00:07:27,196 --> 00:07:28,876
in dollars and cents.

142
00:07:28,876 --> 00:07:31,636
But we have been open for about four months now,

143
00:07:31,636 --> 00:07:34,836
and a lot of us have found the library

144
00:07:34,836 --> 00:07:40,396
to be one of the rare places where we feel truly at home.

145
00:07:40,396 --> 00:07:43,836
On most days, our library also doubles as a community center.

146
00:07:43,836 --> 00:07:48,196
So apart from the usual book clubs, women sharing circles,

147
00:07:48,196 --> 00:07:54,676
people have also used it to host curry night, hot pot night

148
00:07:54,676 --> 00:07:56,036
for 40 people.

149
00:07:56,036 --> 00:07:58,036
And also, two times a month

150
00:07:58,036 --> 00:08:01,356
we run a midnight library where we open till late,

151
00:08:01,356 --> 00:08:07,556
or at least as late as the last person would like to stay.

152
00:08:07,556 --> 00:08:11,876
I see community in every corner of our little library.

153
00:08:11,876 --> 00:08:15,876
I see community in the people who bring food and drinks

154
00:08:15,876 --> 00:08:18,156
and ideas and resources to our library

155
00:08:18,156 --> 00:08:20,436
that they freely share with each other.

156
00:08:20,436 --> 00:08:23,316
People rush to return their library books

157
00:08:23,316 --> 00:08:25,516
even though we have no late fees --

158
00:08:25,516 --> 00:08:28,916
I think because they can sense the love and the care

159
00:08:28,916 --> 00:08:31,596
that has gone into every shelf in our library,

160
00:08:31,596 --> 00:08:35,356
and also because I think they just really want to share the books

161
00:08:35,356 --> 00:08:36,596
with other people, right?

162
00:08:36,596 --> 00:08:39,756
They want other people to have access to the books.

163
00:08:39,756 --> 00:08:43,676
I see community in the little children who come into our library.

164
00:08:43,676 --> 00:08:47,116
Little kids who actually remind each other to, you know, like,

165
00:08:47,116 --> 00:08:49,596
please take care of the books, don't bend the pages,

166
00:08:49,596 --> 00:08:51,476
put the book back in the right place.

167
00:08:51,476 --> 00:08:53,636
I see community in our volunteer librarians,

168
00:08:53,636 --> 00:08:58,796
who spend a lot of time cleaning the library, cataloging books,

169
00:08:58,796 --> 00:09:04,116
and pouring their soul into making sure that the library is a welcoming space

170
00:09:04,116 --> 00:09:06,516
for everyone.

171
00:09:06,516 --> 00:09:08,156
And because of all this,

172
00:09:08,156 --> 00:09:11,676
Singapore feels a little different to me these days.

173
00:09:11,676 --> 00:09:14,996
These days, Singapore feels a little more generous,

174
00:09:14,996 --> 00:09:18,236
warm, open, kind,

175
00:09:18,236 --> 00:09:21,436
joyful and trusting.

176
00:09:21,436 --> 00:09:24,396
In a world where people feel increasingly lonely,

177
00:09:24,396 --> 00:09:27,516
society feels increasingly fragmented,

178
00:09:27,516 --> 00:09:29,476
this little library has given us --

179
00:09:29,476 --> 00:09:33,636
or at least it has given me -- a feeling of power.

180
00:09:33,636 --> 00:09:36,716
Not the kind of power that is heavy and destructive,

181
00:09:36,716 --> 00:09:40,476
but the kind of power that is light and regenerative.

182
00:09:40,476 --> 00:09:43,196
This little library has given me renewed hope

183
00:09:43,196 --> 00:09:47,276
that we do have the power to come together

184
00:09:47,276 --> 00:09:51,196
to build what we think is missing in our lives,

185
00:09:51,196 --> 00:09:54,036
in our city, in our world.

186
00:09:54,036 --> 00:09:58,196
And sometimes, that missing thing is a small, shared,

187
00:09:58,196 --> 00:10:00,356
community-owned library.

188
00:10:00,356 --> 00:10:04,356
Thank you.
